## Done — bug fixed and verified

**Root cause** (`swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:4`): `extractTags` split the text on spaces and only treated a `#` as a tag when it started a whitespace-delimited token. So a `#` glued to preceding punctuation (`list,#urgent`) or nested inside `(#ideas)` never became its own token and was dropped.

**Fix:** replaced the split/filter approach with a regex scan for `#[A-Za-z0-9_]+`, matching a `#` followed by word characters anywhere in the string. Matches come back left-to-right, so tag order is preserved; a trailing `#` with no following word char yields nothing.

**Ad-hoc verification** (not suite green): I built a temporary Swift package at `/private/var/folders/1s/ggz8433s6x56bzrwdtfbblfc0000gn/T/hermes-verify-notekit/` that depends on the real, changed NoteKit source and ran a small executable against it. All cases passed:

- `shopping list,#urgent.` → `["urgent"]` ✅
- `(#ideas)` → `["ideas"]` ✅
- `(#ideas) and #urgent, #notes` → `["ideas", "urgent", "notes"]` ✅ (order preserved)
- Existing behavior intact: `hello #world #swift` → `["world", "swift"]`, `#a #b #c` ordered, `#dup #dup` → `["dup", "dup"]`, plain text / trailing `#` → `[]` ✅
- Original fixture suite (`swift test --disable-sandbox`) still passes.

The temp package was deleted afterward; no notekit artifacts remain. The fixture test file was left untouched.

Note: `swift` requires `--disable-sandbox` in this environment (`sandbox-exec: Operation not permitted` otherwise) — an environment limitation, not a code issue.

